  "number": 5,
  "title": "Silent Partner",
  "tagline": "A band back together, a song with one name on it, and a fall in the small hours.",
  "setting": "Hafod · mid-Wales · October 2024",
  "sketchTitle": "HAFOD",
  "sketchCredit": "SKETCH: SITE VISIT, 30 OCTOBER 2024 · NOT TO SCALE",
  "stayLabel": "Stay at Hafod",
  "briefing": [
    "Hafod Studios, mid-Wales. Wednesday 30 October 2024. A hill farm above the Wye, at the end of a single-track lane: a farmhouse, an old stone granary, and a long barn that has been a recording studio since 1979. Half the records you remember from the nineties were made here.",
    "In the early hours of Sunday 27 October, Rhys Morgan, 52, the singer of Halflight, fell backwards down the stone steps outside the granary where he was sleeping. His smartwatch felt the fall and rang 999 by itself. He was dead before the ambulance arrived.",
    "It was meant to be a comeback. Halflight split up in 1998; this week, for the first time in twenty-six years, the three of them were back in one room, recording, with a documentary camera watching. Their old hit, ‘Small Hours’, has been everywhere this year.",
    "The police have the documentary’s footage: Dean Pritchard, the band’s guitarist, shouting at Rhys at the foot of those steps and shoving him in the chest. Dean says he wrote the music of ‘Small Hours’, and he never got the credit. He told the police he had gone to bed; then that he had left Rhys alive ‘at about quarter to two’. The watch felt the fall at 01:41. Dean has been arrested on suspicion of murder and released on bail. The CPS is deciding whether to charge him, and a tabloid has run the pictures under the headline ‘THE SHOVE’.",
    "Dean’s daughter, Ffion, has hired you. ‘Dad’s been angry with Rhys for thirty years. He’s also the only person who never stopped loving him. He didn’t do this.’ Huw Bevan, who owns Hafod, has given you the run of the place, and everyone who was there that night is still close by."
  ],
